    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094437",
      "id": "q-6730",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-6730",
        "knowledge_point_id": "d0bda136-40a9-576a-b85c-4d4ebefaeb27",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent to a Parametric Curve at a Given Point",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094437/assets/9434dda63c08fc1202d16ab5410fe7061050515876ffa473d8720db39079af3e.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$",
        "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}+2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2}{3(2)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
