    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14094452",
      "id": "q-20512",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-20512",
        "knowledge_point_id": "739b1cbc-c1a7-547d-a9fb-1f244bd36827",
        "knowledge_point": "Solving a 3x3 System of Equations Using Standard Gaussian Elimination",
        "problem": "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & 2x+3y+4z=6 \\\\  & 3x+3y+4z=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and hence solve the linear system.",
        "worked_solution": "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+2z=1 \\\\  & y=4 \\\\  & z=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can apply back substitution. Substituting both $y=4$ and $z=-4$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+(4)+2(-4)=1\\,⟹\\,x=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5,y=4$, and $z=-4.$ Or, in vector form, $⟨5,4,-4⟩.$",
        "answer_fields": [
