      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-109221",
        "knowledge_point_id": "866ac63c-b20a-55fd-a1b5-29400355f195",
        "knowledge_point": "Computing the Expected Value of a Scaled Random Variable",
        "problem": "A random variable $X$ has the probability distribution given below.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $\\text{E}[-2X].$",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's find $\\text{E}[X].$ \n\nThe expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ and sample space $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nSumming up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.35+1\\cdot 0.15+2\\cdot 0.15+3\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[-2X] & =-2\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 1.75 \\\\  & =-3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "answer_fields": [
