        "problem": "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-3+7t \\\\ y=5t \\\\ z=1-5t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line.",
        "worked_solution": "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3+7t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 1-5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the line passes through the point $(-3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$",
